  "overview": {
    "text": "The associative property, also called associativity, describes how a binary operation handles grouping: for three inputs a, b, and c, (a ★ b) ★ c = a ★ (b ★ c). The inputs stay in the same order; only the parentheses move, so this differs from commutativity, which changes their order. For real-number addition and multiplication, regrouping leaves the sum or product unchanged—for example, (7 + 8) + 2 = 7 + (8 + 2). This can make calculations easier by letting you combine convenient pairs first. Subtraction and division are not associative, so regrouping them can change the answer. Associativity also matters beyond arithmetic: it is one of the requirements for a group in abstract algebra.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://openstax.org/books/elementary-algebra/pages/1-9-properties-of-real-numbers",
      "https://openstax.org/books/intermediate-algebra/pages/1-5-properties-of-real-numbers",
      "https://math.ucla.edu/~sharifi/notes/algebra-ch02.html"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "In the general rule, ★ stands for an operation, and the same three inputs remain in the same order as the parentheses shift.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/elementary-algebra/pages/1-9-properties-of-real-numbers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Real-number addition and multiplication are associative: (a + b) + c = a + (b + c), and (a · b) · c = a · (b · c).",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/elementary-algebra/pages/1-9-properties-of-real-numbers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Associativity is about grouping; commutativity is about changing the order of the inputs.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/elementary-algebra/pages/1-9-properties-of-real-numbers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Subtraction and division are not associative: (10 − 3) − 2 = 5, while 10 − (3 − 2) = 9.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/intermediate-algebra/pages/1-5-properties-of-real-numbers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In abstract algebra, associativity is one of the defining requirements for a group, alongside an identity element and inverses.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://math.ucla.edu/~sharifi/notes/algebra-ch02.html"
      ]
    }
  ],
